\f1\fs22 <\'97 some stuff about Hayley\'92s trip to work \'85 her apartment is above the Layer but, on a bad day, like this day, the wind would lick up tufts of it and waft it at her barren balcony, always sunless anyway, because neighbouring buildings and sprouts of hover-copt pads on her own residential tower, cast it in perpetual shadow \'85 she took the lift up and walked the web of pedestrian bridges to the Rail (she couldn\'92t afford a hover-copt), took a carriage for work until her tablet bleated a series of warnings: she\'92d spent quite enough on transport that month, she needed to up her walking if she was to avoid dying seven years early, and there was a special on breakfast burritos at the next stop, which, if she didn\'92t exploit, the bonus rate for her savings account would be cancelled this month, not that she had much in it anyway. She dutifully got off the Rail, bought the burrito, and tossed it off the next pedestrian bridge, knowing the Layer would take care of it, dissolving every grisly ligament of it before it hit the ground. The last part of her trip is taking a bus-sized elevator down to Police Central\'92s office.\
